## Solution

**Goal.** Evaluate $I = \displaystyle\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{\ln(x^2+1)}{1+x^2}\,dx$.

**Setup.** Factor $1+z^2 = (z-i)(z+i)$ and write
$$\ln(1+z^2) = \log(z+i) + \log(z-i).$$
Consider the meromorphic function
$$f(z) = \frac{\log(z+i)}{z^2+1} = \frac{\log(z+i)}{(z-i)(z+i)},$$
where $\log$ is the branch of the logarithm with branch cut along the ray $\{-i - t : t \ge 0\}$ (the negative imaginary axis below $-i$). This cut lies entirely in the **lower** half-plane, so $\log(z+i)$ is holomorphic in the closed upper half-plane (note $z+i$ never lands on the cut there, since for $\operatorname{Im} z \ge 0$ we have $\operatorname{Im}(z+i) \ge 1 > 0$).

**Contour.** Let $C_R$ be the closed contour consisting of $[-R,R]$ on the real axis plus the upper semicircle $\Gamma_R$ of radius $R$, traversed counterclockwise. For $R > 1$, the only singularity of $f$ inside $C_R$ is the simple pole at $z = i$ (the point $z = -i$ is both outside the contour and is the branch point of the logarithm, lying on the cut).

**Residue at $z = i$.**
$$\operatorname{Res}_{z=i} f(z) = \frac{\log(i+i)}{(i+i)} = \frac{\log(2i)}{2i}.$$
Since $2i$ lies on the positive imaginary axis, $\log(2i) = \ln 2 + i\frac{\pi}{2}$ (principal value). Hence
$$\operatorname{Res}_{z=i} f(z) = \frac{\ln 2 + i\pi/2}{2i}.$$

**Residue theorem.**
$$\oint_{C_R} f(z)\,dz = 2\pi i \cdot \frac{\ln 2 + i\pi/2}{2i} = \pi\ln 2 + i\,\frac{\pi^2}{2}.$$

**Arc estimate.** On $\Gamma_R$, $|z| = R$, so $|z^2+1| \ge R^2 - 1$ and $|\log(z+i)| \le \ln|z+i| + \pi \le \ln(R+1) + \pi$. Thus
$$\left|\int_{\Gamma_R} f(z)\,dz\right| \le \frac{\ln(R+1)+\pi}{R^2-1}\cdot \pi R \;\xrightarrow{R\to\infty}\; 0.$$
Therefore
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{\log(x+i)}{x^2+1}\,dx = \pi\ln 2 + i\,\frac{\pi^2}{2}. \tag{$\ast$}$$

**Extracting the real part.** For real $x$, $x+i$ has positive imaginary part, so
$$\log(x+i) = \ln|x+i| + i\arg(x+i) = \tfrac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+1) + i\arg(x+i).$$
A direct computation gives $\arg(x+i) = \frac{\pi}{2} - \arctan x$ for all real $x$ (verified at $x=0$: $\pi/2$; as $x\to+\infty$: $0$; as $x\to-\infty$: $\pi$). Hence
$$\log(x+i) = \tfrac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+1) + i\!\left(\tfrac{\pi}{2} - \arctan x\right).$$

Substituting into $(\ast)$ and taking the **real part**:
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+1)}{x^2+1}\,dx = \pi\ln 2.$$

(The imaginary part is consistent too: $\int \frac{\pi/2}{x^2+1}dx = \frac{\pi}{2}\cdot\pi = \frac{\pi^2}{2}$, and $\int \frac{\arctan x}{x^2+1}dx = 0$ since the integrand is odd, giving imaginary part $\frac{\pi^2}{2}$, matching $(\ast)$.)

**Conclusion.**
$$\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{\ln(x^2+1)}{1+x^2}\,dx = 2\pi\ln 2.}$$

